% ============================================================================
% Constraint C11: status/revocation-propagation mismatch  [DRAFT - unverified, needs check -k]
% ============================================================================
% Paper C11 / vault C-036 (static-dynamic-revocation-mismatch), the taxonomy
% instrument for the status/revocation-propagation design-error class (C-045).
% Two claims with mismatched revocation lifespans must not share a credential:
% revoking a dynamic claim would needlessly revoke a co-located static one.
% Dual of C10 (dependent-claim bundling, C-035), which forces co-location.
%
% DRAFT status and open modeling questions (no solver available this session):
%   - The base metamodel (vc_metamodel.refinery) has NO revocation/lifecycle
%     structure. This file adds the minimal new structure: two marker classes
%     (StaticRevocation, DynamicRevocation) each targeting a Claim. Whether
%     revocation cadence belongs under GovernanceAnnotation, its own annotation
%     family, or as a Claim attribute is UNRESOLVED; kept standalone here to
%     avoid mislabeling it FSL (GovernanceAnnotation is in the FSL shadow pred).
%   - "Same credential" for two claims is NOT expressible as a shared source:
%     Claim declares `contains CredEntity[1] source`, so a CredEntity has at
%     most one container and two distinct claims can never share one source
%     (Refinery containment; this is why the CSOK instance gives each claim its
%     own CredentialSubject, related only by the `aligned` shadow pred). Two
%     claims are co-located instead when both are reachable from the same
%     credential root through the claim graph (`cred_neighbors+`, the chained
%     shape the ED experiment builds). The traversal below is the intended
%     idiom but is UNVERIFIED.
%   - UNEXERCISED: the current CSOK instances hold exactly one claim per
%     credential, so this error fires on nothing there. A firing instance (one
%     credential root reaching both a static- and a dynamic-marked claim) must
%     be added and the whole checked with `refinery check -k` before this is
%     trusted. See the commented sketch at the end of this file.

import vc_metamodel.

% Revocation-pattern markers on claims (minimal added structure).
class StaticRevocation {
    Claim[1] target
}

class DynamicRevocation {
    Claim[1] target
}

% Claim membership: cl belongs to the credential rooted at CredentialSubject r
% if its source is r itself or a CredEntity reachable from r through the
% credential's claim graph (cred_neighbors, chained credentials).  [UNVERIFIED]
pred claim_in_credential(Claim cl, CredentialSubject r) <->
    Claim::source(cl, r)
  ; Claim::source(cl, x), cred_neighbors+(r, x).

% C11: a credential must not bundle claims of mismatched revocation cadence.
% Two distinct claims reachable from one credential root, one marked static
% and one marked dynamic, are co-located in one credential -> error.  [UNVERIFIED]
error revocation_mismatch(Claim cl1, Claim cl2) <->
    cl1 != cl2,
    claim_in_credential(cl1, r),
    claim_in_credential(cl2, r),
    StaticRevocation::target(_, cl1),
    DynamicRevocation::target(_, cl2).

% --- Firing-scenario sketch (commented; needs a two-claim credential) ---
% A future session must build an instance where one credential root reaches two
% claims (a chained credential per the ED family), one StaticRevocation-marked
% and one DynamicRevocation-marked, then run `refinery check -k` to confirm this
% error fires (expected UNSAT), and confirm a single-cadence bundle stays SAT.
% Building that instance requires the chained-credential scaffolding; see the
% csok_instance_2x / _3x and evaluation/generate_instances.py chained emitters.
